body:
The new authentication paths are untested end to end:
- backup-code login in `authorize`;
- consumption of a used code, so reuse must fail;
- disabling 2FA with a backup code.

The PR only exercises the download and copy buttons and leaves a TODO. The existing assertion `expect(page.locator(...).isChecked()).toBeTruthy()` checks a Promise and is always truthy, as the added FIXME admits. These are security-relevant flows and should have coverage before merging.

Any anonymous request to /embed/best with an unknown `embed_url` enqueues a RetrieveTopic job. The only guards are:
- the Referer header, which is trivially spoofed outside a browser;
- a URL-host check against `embeddable_host`;
- a Redis throttle keyed on the full URL string.

An attacker can vary the path or query string (`?x=1`, `?x=2`, ...) to bypass the throttle. Each variant then fetches the remote page via `open()`, can trigger a full synchronous feed poll (`Jobs::PollFeed.new.execute`), and creates a brand-new topic through `TopicEmbed.import_remote`. The result is unbounded topic spam and job/HTTP load from unauthenticated traffic. The URL should be normalized (or limited to known URLs), and creation should be rate limited per client.

The PR changes how translations are resolved in every environment: a custom fallback list, explicit locale loading, and removal of the production config. It adds no specs covering:
- the fallback order (user locale → site locale → en);
- the interaction with the translation cache;
- `ensure_loaded!`.

The PR adds a custom fallback order (user locale, then site locale, then English) and lazy loading of fallback locales. No specs cover either.

Untested cases include:
- a key missing in the user locale falling back to the site default, then to :en;
- interaction with the translation cache;
- loading of fallback locales outside controllers.

The new helper returns true only when `user != null`. Before this change, the conditional-UI webauthn data was filled precisely when the user was NOT yet identified (`context.getUser() == null`).

The helper is now used in `authenticate()` (line 115) and in `challenge(context, error, field)` (line 137). As a result, the initial login page, where no user is set, no longer gets `fillContextForm` called. That means:
- no challenge auth note is stored;
- no `ENABLE_WEBAUTHN_CONDITIONAL_UI` attribute is set;
- the error re-render after a bad username or password also loses passkeys.

Normal passkey login stops working, and tests like `webauthnLoginWithDiscoverableKey` / `passwordLoginWithNonDiscoverableKey`, which expect `form#webauth` on first load, would fail. The check should not exclude the `user == null` case; most likely it should drop the user condition entirely.

The listener now only runs when V1 `ADMIN_FINE_GRAINED_AUTHZ` is enabled. Under V2 nothing cleans up when a client is deleted:
- the per-client resource (named after the client id) stays in the admin-permissions client;
- scope permissions referencing that resource stay as well;
- those permissions keep pointing at a non-existent client.

If V1 and V2 are both enabled, `management()` returns `MgmtPermissionsV2`. Its `clients().setPermissionsEnabled` throws `UnsupportedOperationException`, so deleting a client would fail.

All the new tests only check that violations are reported. None checks that valid input produces no messages, for example:
- an identical anchor in the translation;
- allowed tags like `<br />` or `<b>` when the English value has HTML;
- entities like `&laquo;`;
- `{{x}}` placeholders.

The verifier's unescape and comment-stripping normalization is fragile. Without negative tests, false positives that break the build would go unnoticed.

`_get_event_stats` passes the outer `dataset` and the raw `request` into `is_errors_query_for_error_upsampled_projects`. It does not use the `scoped_dataset` and `query` arguments it actually runs. In the dashboard split paths, the dataset is switched to `discover` and the query is rewritten, e.g. `({query}) AND !event.type:transaction` or `AND event.type:transaction`. The upsampling decision is still made from the original request, so it can be wrong in both directions:
- errors-only split queries are never upsampled when the outer dataset is metrics-enhanced;
- a transactions-only split query can be upsampled if the user's query text contained `event.type:error`.

There are no tests for:
- `is_errors_query_for_error_upsampled_projects`, including cache hit/miss behaviour and stale entries;
- negated queries such as `!event.type:error`;
- the dashboard split paths.

These are exactly where the bugs above live. The `snuba_params` fixture built in setUp is never used.

The core new behaviour has no test coverage:
- authenticating with a backup code in `next-auth-options`;
- rejecting a reused code;
- rejecting a wrong code;
- disabling 2FA with a backup code.
The PR leaves this as a TODO.

The PR also documents a FIXME at line 50 instead of fixing it. `expect(page.locator(...).isChecked()).toBeTruthy()` asserts on a Promise, so it always passes. In that test the backup-codes dialog is never closed, so `onEnable` is never invoked, yet the test still passes.

Add tests for backup-code login (including that a second use of the same code fails) and for disabling with a backup code. Fix the always-true assertion with `await expect(locator).toBeChecked()`.

A slot is rejected only if it ends at or before the override start, or starts strictly after the override end. Slots that straddle a boundary are accepted:
- a slot that starts before the override start but ends inside it;
- a slot that starts inside the override and ends after it (e.g. override 14:00–18:00, 60-min slot at 17:30);
- a slot that starts exactly at the override end.

For round-robin events, slots are generated from other hosts' availability, so this lets bookings extend outside the fixed host's override window. The check should require `slotStart >= overrideStart && slotEnd <= overrideEnd`.

The only new test repeats the single-user override scenario with a +6:00 invitee. Its IST schedule doesn't cross UTC midnight, and the override day has no bookings.

Nothing tests the headline fix: a round-robin event with a fixed host's date override. Nothing covers the risky new paths either:
- busy times or bookings on an override day (currently ignored);
- multiple override ranges on one day;
- working hours that span UTC midnight (e.g. a US timezone);
- overrides that shift across the invitee's midnight.

These gaps let the serious regressions in `checkIfIsAvailable` and `getSlots` pass CI.

The only added test re-runs the personal-event date-override scenario with a second, east-of-UTC timezone. Nothing covers the PR's headline change: a round-robin event with a fixed host whose date override should restrict slots.

The new logic in `checkIfIsAvailable` is also untested for:
- a booking or busy time on a date-override day (which would have exposed the busy-check bypass);
- users with split working hours or hours crossing UTC midnight (e.g. any US timezone);
- slots near the end of an override.

All fixtures use Asia/Kolkata 9:30–18:00, which maps to a single UTC range and hides these regressions.

The format regex requires a dot-separated name ending in a 2–5 letter TLD. That rejects:
- longer TLDs such as `.museum`, `.technology` or `.online`;
- `localhost`;
- IP addresses;
- internationalized/punycode TLDs (`xn--...`).

The previous site setting accepted any host. Admins can no longer add these hosts. Hosts carried over by the migration (which skips validation) cannot be edited and re-saved from the new admin UI without failing validation.

The only controller specs check the class hierarchy. Nothing exercises:
- the create/update/destroy actions;
- the uncategorized fallback for a blank `category_id`;
- error rendering on invalid hosts;
- the embedding show endpoint and its serializer output that the admin UI depends on.

Nor is there any spec for the data migration, which contains the riskiest logic in this PR.

The only controller specs added check subclassing. Nothing covers `create`/`update`/`destroy` on `Admin::EmbeddableHostsController`. Specifically, nothing tests:
- the uncategorized default for a blank `category_id`;
- host normalization through the endpoint;
- error responses for invalid input.

The nil-host and missing-record crashes noted above would have been caught by basic request specs.

No specs were added or updated for:
- the new downsize loop: images over the limit get shrunk, files that cannot shrink are still rejected, non-images are skipped;
- the changed `OptimizedImage.resize`/`downsize`/`optimize` signatures;
- the removed `OptimizedImage.dimensions`.

The duplicate `downsize` definition would likely have been caught by a spec exercising the width/height form.

The spec only passes `unsubscribe_url` to keep the existing test from raising. It never asserts that the per-topic URL appears in the body or HTML part.

There are also no tests for:
- the new `TopicsController#unsubscribe` action: level transitions, missing TopicUser, slug redirect, logged-out behavior;
- `Topic#unsubscribe_url`;
- the `render :show` change in `perform_show_response`.

That last one introduced the canonical-URL regression, which is currently untested.

`rss.items.each` has no per-item error handling, so the first exception stops all remaining items on every run. Likely triggers include:
- a nil `content`;
- a deleted embed post;
- an `embed_url` longer than the 255-char varchar column;
- a unique-index race;
- `RateLimiter::LimitExceeded` when `embed_by_username` is not staff.

On the last point: topic creation is still rate-limited (e.g. `rate_limit_create_topic` of 5s) despite `skip_validations`, so a non-staff import user will typically fail on the second new item.

Because the same items come first on every hourly run, the feed can be permanently stuck. Rescue and log per item, and bypass or disable rate limiting for the import user, as the Disqus task does.

The PR adds a custom fallback order (user locale → site default → en), changes the loading path (`ensure_loaded!`), and turns fallbacks on in every environment, including test and development. No tests cover any of it.

There is no check that:
- a key missing in the user locale resolves from the site default locale, then from English;
- the fallback locales get loaded;
- cached translations behave correctly when the default locale changes.

Regressions such as the cache and loading issues in this change would go unnoticed.

The PR changes translation behavior in every environment. Fallbacks were previously enabled only in production and are now on everywhere. It also adds `FallbackLocaleList` and `I18n.ensure_loaded!`, but no tests cover them.

There is no spec checking that:
- a key missing in the user locale resolves to the site default locale and then to English;
- duplicates are removed when the user locale equals the site default;
- the fallback locales get loaded under the lazy-loading patch.

Regressions in any of these would go unnoticed.

The PR changes translation resolution for every environment: user locale, then site default locale, then `en`. It also adds `I18n.ensure_loaded!` and `FallbackLocaleList`. No specs cover any of this:
- that a key missing in the user locale resolves from the site default and then from `en`;
- that `ensure_loaded!` loads all fallback locales;
- that results behave correctly with the translation cache.

Regressions such as the cache and lazy-loading problems above would go unnoticed.

The PR's goal is to let a user access a resource as soon as the permission exists, by re-querying the DB when cached permissions don't allow access. However, the denial cache is checked first and short-circuits that re-query for the full 30s TTL.

Any denial recorded for a (user, action, name, folder) tuple keeps the user locked out after the permission is granted. Examples:
- an existence probe or GET on a UID before the dashboard was created;
- a check that raced with the default creator permissions being written;
- an admin granting access right after a 403.

The new 'requery on miss' path never runs in those cases. Consider not caching denials for resources that may be newly created, using a much shorter TTL, or invalidating denial entries when permissions are refetched.

When a cached `IdentityProviderListQuery` lacks the requested org's searchKey, the code does three things:
- calls `cache.invalidateObject(cacheKey)`, which bumps the revision itself;
- builds a new query that copies all searches from the previously read `query`;
- adds it with `cache.getCurrentCounter()` as the startup revision, which bypasses the startup-revision guard.

If a cluster or local invalidation of this key, e.g. another transaction hiding or unpublishing an IDP, lands between `cache.get(cacheKey)` and `invalidateObject`, it is effectively overwritten. The new entry's revision matches and is accepted, yet it carries the stale sets of every other organization from the old object. Because one entry per FetchMode is shared by all organizations, a single race can resurrect stale login lists for all orgs. Ineligible IDPs would then keep appearing until the next invalidation; only deleted IDPs are self-healed via the `getById == null` check.

`testManageOnlyOneClient` makes several changes to `myclient`, the client that `realmAdminClient` authenticates with:
- removes `defaultClientScopes.get(1)` (index into an unordered list, so the removed scope is nondeterministic, possibly `basic` or `roles`);
- re-adds it as optional;
- renames the client.

None of this is restored. Other tests in the class reuse the same realm and client, so results can depend on test execution order and on which scope happened to be removed. `testMapRolesAndCompositesOnlyOneClient` similarly leaves created client roles and a role mapping on myadmin. Clean these up or mark the realm dirty.

`canView()` only checks the manage-clients/view-clients roles and the all-clients VIEW scope.

An admin granted all-clients MANAGE gets:
- `canManage()` and `canManageClientScopes()` true;
- `canView(ClientScopeModel)` true, since it explicitly accepts MANAGE;
- `canView()`, and therefore `canViewClientScopes()`, `requireView()` and `requireViewClientScopes()`, false.

Such an admin can modify things (for example realm default client scopes) that the view-guarded endpoints refuse to show them. Manage should imply view here, as it does in `canView(ClientScopeModel)` and via `canConfigure` in `canView(ClientModel)`.

`testRecoveryKeysSetupAndLogin` signs in once with code #0 and stops. The backing provider's `isValid` (BackwardsCompatibilityUserStorage line 340) accepts any of the generated codes via `anyMatch` and never consumes them. The test would therefore still pass if the wrong code were entered, if a code could be reused, or if the code number shown by the login page were wrong.

The new user-storage paths in `RecoveryAuthnCodesFormAuthenticator` (all-codes-used handling, the required action being re-added) and the precedence between federated and local credentials in `RecoveryAuthnCodesUtils.getCredential` have no coverage.

Suggested additions:
- a wrong-code rejection check;
- a second login to verify the code is consumed;
- a case where the user already has local codes before storage support.

No tests were added for:
- `OptimizedCursorPaginator`;
- the `optimized_pagination=true` endpoint path;
- the modified offset handling in `BasePaginator.get_result`, which affects every endpoint using it.

Even a basic test that requests the audit log endpoint with `optimized_pagination=true` and one entry would have exposed the `TypeError` from `math.floor(datetime)`. A test with a negative-offset cursor would have exposed the `ValueError` from QuerySet negative slicing.

The unit tests cover only the private helpers. `is_errors_query_for_error_upsampled_projects`, including its cache hit/miss logic, is never called; `self.snuba_params` is built but unused.

There are no tests for:
- negated or compound queries (`!event.type:error`, `event.type:error OR ...`), which currently misbehave;
- the errors dataset through the endpoint;
- top-events requests;
- multiple yAxis values;
- allowlist changes taking effect, which the cache delays.

The endpoint tests mock `options` entirely, so the real option registration and lookup path is not covered.

`_is_error_focused_query` returns True whenever the lowercased query contains the substring `event.type:error`. That also matches:
- `!event.type:error`, which explicitly excludes errors;
- `event.type:error OR event.type:transaction`;
- any free text containing that string.

For the discover dataset, such a query gets `count()` rewritten to `upsampled_count()`. The results then mix or contain non-error rows (e.g. transactions) whose `sample_weight` is missing, producing wrong counts.

The check also reads `request.GET["query"]` instead of the query actually being executed. The parsed search filters should be inspected for a positive `event.type:error` term rather than doing a substring match on the raw string.

The tests cover only the private helpers. `is_errors_query_for_error_upsampled_projects` (the function the endpoint calls, including the cache path) is never exercised, and the `self.snuba_params` built in `setUp` is unused.

Several cases are also untested:
- negated or mixed queries such as `!event.type:error`;
- top-events requests with upsampling;
- events lacking `sample_weight` in allowlisted projects.

The endpoint tests mock `options` but would not detect stale-cache behaviour.

If `Integration.objects.get(external_id=installation_id)` raises DoesNotExist, the view goes straight to `pipeline.next_step()` without comparing `github_authenticated_user` to anything. `build_integration` then fetches the installation using the app JWT, which can read any installation of the app. It links the installation to the current Sentry org.

The attack this PR targets (supplying someone else's installation_id) therefore still works whenever no Integration row exists yet. Examples:
- the window before the installation webhook is processed;
- failed or delayed webhook deliveries;
- self-hosted instances that don't receive GitHub webhooks.

GitHub installation IDs are sequential and easy to enumerate. A stronger check is to use the OAuth token from `OAuthLoginView` to confirm the installation appears in the user's `GET /user/installations`, and do that in every branch.

An `assignment_source` positional-or-keyword parameter was added to the abstract `sync_status_outbound`, but:
- no caller passes it;
- the status-sync task and kick-off path are unchanged;
- concrete implementations (Jira, VSTS, etc.) don't declare it.

The base signature now suggests status sync cycles are handled when they are not. The parameter also sits in the 4th positional slot, which subclasses don't accept positionally. Either implement status-sync cycle breaking end to end or drop this signature change.

```
try {
    await sendAddGuestsEmails(evt, guests);
} catch (err) {
    console.log("Error sending AddGuestsEmails");
}
```
The actual error (`err`) is discarded and only a generic string is logged via `console.log` rather than the project's structured logger. This makes it impossible to diagnose email delivery failures in production and is inconsistent with the rest of the codebase, which generally uses `@calcom/lib/logger`.

```
onError: (err) => {
      const message = `${err.data?.code}: ${t(err.message)}`;
      showToast(message || t("unable_to_add_guests"), "error");
},
```
`message` is built from a template string so it is always truthy, meaning the `t("unable_to_add_guests")` fallback can never actually be shown. Additionally, `t(err.message)` passes the raw TRPC error message (which may not be a translation key, e.g. generic validation errors) directly into the translation function, which can display an un-translated raw string to users.

The new helper function:

```go
func enableSqlExpressions(h *ExpressionQueryReader) bool {
	enabled := !h.features.IsEnabledGlobally(featuremgmt.FlagSqlExpressions)
	if enabled {
		return false
	}
	return false
}
```

computes a local variable named `enabled` as the *negation* of the feature flag state, then unconditionally returns `false` from both branches regardless of that variable's value. The function name and the variable name both suggest the SQL-expressions flag state is being consulted, but the flag value has zero effect on the result — the function always returns `false`. This is extremely easy for a future maintainer to misread as 'enabled mirrors the flag' and to 'simplify' into `return enabled` (which would silently re-enable the exact RCE/LFI vector this PR is meant to close) or to assume toggling `FlagSqlExpressions` has some effect when it does not. There is no comment explaining that this is an intentional hard kill-switch, which is a serious risk for a security-critical code path.

GroupPermissionsV2.canManage() (no-arg, used e.g. by GroupsResource.addTopLevelGroup() via auth.groups().requireManage()) is implemented as:

    return hasPermission(null, AdminPermissionsSchema.VIEW, AdminPermissionsSchema.MANAGE);

This is an exact copy of canView()'s scope list. As a result, an admin who was only granted the VIEW scope on the all-groups resource (and not MANAGE) is treated as being allowed to manage (create/update) groups, because hasPermission() returns true as soon as any of the listed scopes is granted. canManage(GroupModel) correctly checks only AdminPermissionsSchema.MANAGE, which highlights that the no-arg canManage() is inconsistent/incorrect. This is a privilege-escalation bug: a read-only group permission effectively becomes a write permission for top-level group management. No test exercises a VIEW-only permission against a management action, so this regression is not caught.

In the constructor:
```
Objects.requireNonNull(grantType, "Null grantType not allowed");
Objects.requireNonNull(grantType, "Null rawTokenId not allowed");
```
The second call re-checks `grantType` again (with a misleading message about `rawTokenId`) instead of checking `rawTokenId`. Consequently, a null `rawTokenId` is never rejected. Downstream, `DefaultTokenContextEncoderProvider.encodeTokenId()` concatenates `tokenContext.getRawTokenId()` directly into the token id string; if it is null, the resulting access token id will literally contain the string "null" instead of a real token id, corrupting token uniqueness/identity instead of failing fast with a clear error.

In GroupPermissionsV2.canManage() (no-arg, global check), the implementation is:
```
public boolean canManage() {
    if (root.hasOneAdminRole(AdminRoles.MANAGE_USERS)) return true;
    return hasPermission(null, AdminPermissionsSchema.VIEW, AdminPermissionsSchema.MANAGE);
}
```
It checks for either VIEW or MANAGE scope on the all-groups resource, instead of only MANAGE (as correctly done in the per-group overload `canManage(GroupModel group)`, which only checks `AdminPermissionsSchema.MANAGE`). As a result, an admin who was only granted the 'view' scope on the all-groups ('Groups') resource (intended for read-only access) will also satisfy `canManage()`/`requireManage()`. This method is called directly by `GroupsResource.addTopLevelGroup()` via `auth.groups().requireManage()`, so a user with only VIEW permission on groups would be able to create top-level groups - an unintended privilege escalation. The analogous Users V2 implementation (`UserPermissionsV2.canManage(user)`) correctly checks only MANAGE, confirming this is a copy/paste mistake from `canView()`.

In the AccessTokenContext constructor, the fourth Objects.requireNonNull call uses the `grantType` variable again instead of `rawTokenId`:
```
Objects.requireNonNull(grantType, "Null grantType not allowed");
Objects.requireNonNull(grantType, "Null rawTokenId not allowed");
```
As written, `rawTokenId` is never validated for null. If a caller passes a null rawTokenId (e.g. a bug in a custom TokenContextEncoderProvider, or future refactors), the AccessTokenContext will silently store a null rawTokenId, which will later be concatenated in `encodeTokenId()` producing a token id ending in the literal string "null" instead of failing fast with a clear error message.

In `isAccessTokenId(String expectedGrantShortcut)`, the matcher does:
```
if (items[0].substring(3, 5).equals(expectedGrantShortcut)) return false;
return isUUID().matches(items[1]);
```
Two problems: (1) the grant-type shortcut occupies characters 4-5 (0-indexed) of the 6-char context prefix (2 chars session type + 2 chars token type + 2 chars grant type), so `substring(3, 5)` is off by one and will essentially never equal the intended shortcut; (2) even if the indices were correct, the condition is inverted — it returns `false` (no match) when the shortcut *does* match, instead of when it does *not* match. The net effect is that this matcher never actually validates that the token id encodes the expected grant-type shortcut; it effectively degenerates to just checking the UUID portion, silently passing regardless of the grant type encoded. All call sites added in this same file (`expectCodeToToken`, `expectDeviceCodeToToken`, `expectRefresh`, `expectAuthReqIdToToken`) rely on this broken matcher, so the new assertions provide no real verification of the grant-type encoding feature and could mask regressions.

In the `AccessTokenContext` constructor:
```
Objects.requireNonNull(grantType, "Null grantType not allowed");
Objects.requireNonNull(grantType, "Null rawTokenId not allowed");
```
The second line is a copy-paste error: it re-checks `grantType` (with a misleading message) instead of checking `rawTokenId`. As a result, `rawTokenId` is never null-checked, so a null raw token id can be stored in the context and later concatenated into the encoded token id (e.g. producing a literal "null" suffix) or cause an NPE further down the line instead of failing fast with a clear error at construction time.

In the AccessTokenContext constructor, two consecutive Objects.requireNonNull calls both reference `grantType`:
```
Objects.requireNonNull(grantType, "Null grantType not allowed");
Objects.requireNonNull(grantType, "Null rawTokenId not allowed");
```
The second call should validate `rawTokenId`, not `grantType` again. As a result, a null `rawTokenId` is never rejected. If this constructor is ever invoked with a null raw id (directly, or via future callers of this public class), `encodeTokenId()` will silently concatenate the literal string "null" into the generated access-token id (e.g. `onrtac:null`) instead of failing fast, producing a malformed/non-unique token id (a potential jti collision across such tokens) instead of raising a clear error at construction time.

In `postInit()`, shortcuts obtained from `OAuth2GrantTypeFactory.getShortcut()` are inserted into `grantsByShortcuts`/`grantsToShortcuts` without any validation of length or null-ness:
```
String grantShortcut = gtf.getShortcut();
grantsByShortcuts.put(grantShortcut, grantName);
grantsToShortcuts.put(grantName, grantShortcut);
```
The downstream decoder (`DefaultTokenContextEncoderProvider.getTokenContextFromTokenId`) hard-codes an expectation of exactly 6 total characters (2+2+2) and will throw `IllegalArgumentException` for any other length, while `encodeTokenId()` has no such check and will happily build a malformed id for any shortcut length. A custom/third-party `OAuth2GrantTypeFactory` that returns a shortcut other than exactly 2 characters (which the companion Javadoc for `getShortcut()` even encourages by suggesting "3-letters") will successfully mint tokens with mis-sized ids that cannot be correctly decoded later, and a null shortcut will throw `NullPointerException` from the `ConcurrentHashMap.put` call during `postInit()`, aborting factory initialization.

`testRecoveryKeysSetupAndLogin` logs in once, always with the first code (expectedCode 0). It does not cover:
- a wrong code;
- consuming a code and being asked for the next one;
- exhausting all codes, which exercises the `allCodesUsed` and re-configure branch that was modified;
- the fallback to local DB storage for non-federated users.

The delayed-authenticator config alias also has a typo (`delayed-suthenticator-config`).

When a user with `IdentityProvider.CAL` and 2FA enabled later signs in via Google or SAML (lines 694–711), their `identityProvider` is updated to `GOOGLE`/`SAML` while `twoFactorEnabled` remains `true`, and login redirects to `/auth/login?totp=<jwt>` (`totpEmail`).

In `authorize` (lines 113–119), the checks:
```ts
if (user.identityProvider !== IdentityProvider.CAL && !credentials.totpCode) {
  throw new Error(ErrorCode.ThirdPartyIdentityProviderEnabled);
}
```
only exempt `credentials.totpCode` and not `credentials.backupCode`. If such a user attempts to authenticate with a backup code (where `credentials.totpCode` is empty), `authorize` throws `ErrorCode.ThirdPartyIdentityProviderEnabled` before reaching the backup code verification on line 131. Additionally, `ExternalTotpFooter` in `apps/web/pages/auth/login.tsx` (lines 135–143, 180) does not render the "Lost access" button when `totpEmail` is set.

After this PR, `evt.destinationCalendar` can be either a non-empty array (when the event type or the organizer user has a destination calendar) or `null`. The collective branch is:

```ts
if (isTeamEventType && eventType.schedulingType === "COLLECTIVE") {
  evt.destinationCalendar?.push(...teamDestinationCalendars);
}
```

If `evt.destinationCalendar` is `null`, the optional-chained `push` is a no-op. That is exactly the scenario this PR is supposed to support: a COLLECTIVE event whose organizer user has not configured a destination calendar but whose team members have. `teamDestinationCalendars` is collected correctly, but it never reaches `event.destinationCalendar`, so `EventManager.createAllCalendarEvents` falls back to "first connected calendar" and the team hosts are not invited to their calendars. Initialize `destinationCalendar` as `[]` (or fallback to `[]` instead of `null`) and `push` unconditionally.

The fallback line is:

```ts
const [mainHostDestinationCalendar] = evt.destinationCalendar ?? [];
if (evt.location === MeetLocationType && mainHostDestinationCalendar.integration !== "google_calendar") {
```

If `evt.destinationCalendar` is `[]` (e.g. for a collective COLLECTIVE booking whose organizer has no destination calendar - the same scenario where the bug above drops the team destinations), then `mainHostDestinationCalendar` is `undefined` and `.integration` throws `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined`. Guard with optional chaining or treat the empty case as "not google_calendar" (current desired behavior is to fall back to Cal Video).

The diff for .topic-meta-data h5 a reads:
-      color: scale-color($primary, $lightness: 30%);
+      color: dark-light-choose(scale-color($primary, $lightness: 70%), scale-color($secondary, $lightness: 30%));

The $lightness argument passed to the $primary branch was changed from 30% to 70%. The PR's stated purpose is to add a $secondary fallback for dark themes without altering the light-theme appearance, but this line now resolves to a much lighter color in light themes. The mobile/topic-post.scss counterpart (line 182 in mobile/topic-post.scss) keeps the original lightness; this case is the only one in the PR where the $primary lightness was accidentally mutated.

The diff reads:
-        color: scale-color($primary, $lightness: 30%);
+        color: dark-light-choose(scale-color($primary, $lightness: 50%), scale-color($secondary, $lightness: 50%));

The $primary lightness was bumped from 30% to 50%, so in light themes the username link now renders noticeably lighter than before. The mobile/user.scss change for the same selector has the identical mistake; the desktop/topic-post.scss .arrow/.highlight-action etc. all keep their original primary lightness, so this looks like an accidental edit rather than an intentional color tweak.

Diff:
-    color: scale-color($primary, $lightness: 20%);
+    color: dark-light-choose(scale-color($primary, $lightness: 50%), scale-color($secondary, $lightness: 50%));

The $primary lightness jumped from 20% to 50%, so the topic-map h3 heading now appears much lighter in light themes than it did on master. Compare with desktop/topic-post.scss line 321, where the equivalent .topic-map h3 was correctly converted keeping the original 20% lightness. This divergence means desktop and mobile render the topic-map title in different shades of $primary, and the mobile light theme visually regresses.

Diff:
-  color: scale-color($primary, $lightness: 70%);
+  color: dark-light-choose(scale-color($primary, $lightness: 30%), scale-color($secondary, $lightness: 70%));

The $primary lightness was dropped from 70% to 30%, so the flag-modal custom-message-length counter text now appears much darker in light themes than before. It is also inconsistent with the equivalent selector in desktop/modal.scss (line 94), which correctly keeps the original 70% primary lightness. The two stylesheets should produce the same color in the light theme for parity between desktop and mobile flagging modals.

In deleteByIDs, when SQLite is used and the configured batch size exceeds the parameter limit, IDs are concatenated into a SQL literal:
    values := fmt.Sprint(ids[0])
    for _, v := range ids[1:] {
        values = fmt.Sprintf("%s, %d", values, v)
    }
Each iteration rebuilds the whole string from scratch, giving O(N^2) time and memory growth proportional to len(ids)^2. With AnnotationCleanupJobBatchSize configured as high as 32767, this produces a string of ~300k+ characters built through tens of thousands of allocations on every cleanup batch. Should use strings.Builder (or similar) to build the IN-list in a single pass. Also, for the typical case where len(ids) is much smaller than the configured batch size (because there are simply few rows to clean), the branch condition checks `r.cfg.AnnotationCleanupJobBatchSize > sqliteParameterLimit` instead of `len(ids) > sqliteParameterLimit`, so the slower inlining path is taken even when the placeholder path would work fine.

The new `enableSqlExpressions` function is:

```go
func enableSqlExpressions(h *ExpressionQueryReader) bool {
    enabled := !h.features.IsEnabledGlobally(featuremgmt.FlagSqlExpressions)
    if enabled {
        return false
    }
    return false
}
```

Both branches return `false`, making the feature flag check and the `if enabled` branch dead code. The function unconditionally returns `false`, ignoring `featuremgmt.FlagSqlExpressions` entirely. The convoluted logic with `!IsEnabledGlobally(...)` is misleading because it suggests the flag is being respected when it isn't. Either the body should be `return false` with a comment explaining this is a security mitigation, or the function should be written to make the unconditional disablement obvious. As-is, this is hard to reason about and any future maintainer might assume the flag is wired up.

Lines 114-115 create two ASN1Encoder instances and call `write()` on each, then discard the results without ever consuming the produced bytes:
```
ASN1Encoder.create().write(rBigInteger);
ASN1Encoder.create().write(sBigInteger);
```
The real DER encoding happens on lines 117-121 via `writeDerSeq(...)`. The two statements above are pure dead code that allocates four throwaway ByteArrayOutputStreams for no purpose. They should be removed.

Authorization allows the request if the user is the organizer OR an attendee:

```ts
const isAttendee = !!booking.attendees.find((attendee) => attendee.email === user.email);
if (!isTeamAdminOrOwner && !isOrganizer && !isAttendee) { ... }
```

Combined with the dialog's unrestricted email entry, any original invitee can inject any third-party email into the booking, getting them full calendar details, organizer info, other attendees, and a meeting invitation without the organizer's consent. The organizer should normally be the only one (plus team admins/owners) permitted to add guests. This is a serious privacy/access-control issue.

The dedupe/blacklist filter compares emails with strict equality:

```ts
const uniqueGuests = guests.filter(
    (guest) =>
      !booking.attendees.some((attendee) => guest === attendee.email) &&
      !blacklistedGuestEmails.includes(guest)
);
```

Email addresses are case-insensitive per RFC 5321. Submitting `Test@Example.com` while an existing attendee is `test@example.com` will not be filtered out, so a duplicate attendee row is created (or causes a unique-constraint failure, surfaced to the user). Both the dedupe check and the blacklist comparison must lowercase values first; same applies to `newGuests.includes(attendee.email)` in `sendAddGuestsEmails` which controls whether new guests get the original 'scheduled' email vs the 'guest added' email.

The handler instantiates the EventManager with the calling session user and their credentials:

```ts
const credentials = await getUsersCredentials(ctx.user);
const eventManager = new EventManager({ ...user, credentials: [...credentials] });
await eventManager.updateCalendarAttendees(evt, booking);
```

When a team admin or attendee (or anyone authorized by the buggy permission check) adds guests, `user` is the requester, not `booking.userId` (the organizer). `updateCalendarAttendees` will then use the requester's calendar integrations to push the updated invite, which can fail, target the wrong calendar, or succeed only partially. Credentials must come from `booking.userId` (or whichever organizer owns the destination calendar).

```ts
try {
  await sendAddGuestsEmails(evt, guests);
} catch (err) {
  console.log("Error sending AddGuestsEmails");
}
```

The exception is dropped (no `logger.error`, no context, no rethrow). The handler still returns `{ message: "Guests added" }`, so the client toasts success and the user assumes guests were notified. At minimum, use `logger.error` with the error and rethrow or return a structured failure so the UI can surface it.

```ts
...calendarEvent.attendees.map((attendee) => {
  if (newGuests.includes(attendee.email)) {
    return sendEmail(() => new AttendeeScheduledEmail(calendarEvent, attendee));
  } else {
    return sendEmail(() => new AttendeeAddGuestsEmail(calendarEvent, attendee));
  }
})
```

`AttendeeScheduledEmail` is the first-touch booking confirmation containing reschedule/cancel links and language framing as if the recipient originally booked it. Newly added guests did not book anything; sending them this template is misleading and may confuse them about whether they own the booking. They should receive `AttendeeAddGuestsEmail` (or a dedicated 'you have been added' template). Additionally, every existing attendee also receives a duplicate `AttendeeAddGuestsEmail` with `icalEvent` `method: REQUEST`, which will trigger another 'invitation' notification in their calendar.

In the recurring-events branch the code does:
```
bookingToDelete.user.credentials.filter(...).forEach(async (credential) => {
  const calendar = await getCalendar(credential);
  for (const updBooking of updatedBookings) {
    ...
    const deletedEvent = await calendar?.deleteEvent(uid, evt, externalCalendarId);
    apiDeletes.push(deletedEvent);
  }
});
```
`forEach` does not await async callbacks, so `apiDeletes` is still (or mostly) empty when execution reaches `await Promise.all(prismaPromises.concat(apiDeletes))` later in the function. The recurring calendar events are not actually deleted; only the local DB rows are cancelled. Use `await Promise.all(...map(async ...))` or a sequential `for...of` loop.

The legacy pre-refactor branch was rewritten from
```
bookingToDelete.user.credentials.filter(...).forEach((credential) => {
  const calendar = getCalendar(credential);
  apiDeletes.push(calendar?.deleteEvent(uid, evt, externalCalendarId) as Promise<unknown>);
});
```
to a `for (const credential of calendarCredentials) { const calendar = await getCalendar(credential); apiDeletes.push(calendar?.deleteEvent(...)); }` loop. Because of the `await`, calendars are now deleted strictly one after another instead of in parallel, multiplying wall-clock time when users have multiple connected calendars.

Inside the recurring-cancellation branch the code does:

  bookingToDelete.user.credentials
    .filter((credential) => credential.type.endsWith("_calendar"))
    .forEach(async (credential) => {
      const calendar = await getCalendar(credential);
      for (const updBooking of updatedBookings) {
        ...
        const deletedEvent = await calendar?.deleteEvent(uid, evt, externalCalendarId);
        apiDeletes.push(deletedEvent);
      }
    });

`forEach` does not await its async callback, so the outer flow continues and `Promise.all(prismaPromises.concat(apiDeletes))` runs before the per-credential async loops have pushed their `deletedEvent` Promises (or worse, after a partial race). For recurring bookings this means many calendar deletion calls are effectively dropped or not waited for, leaving the previous time slot still booked on the user's external calendar. Use `for...of` (matching the non-recurring branch a few lines below) or `Promise.all(calendarCredentials.map(...))`.

`getCalendarCredentials` is still declared as a synchronous function but internally calls the now-async `getCalendar(credential)` without `await`:

  const calendar = getCalendar(credential);
  return app.variant === "calendar" ? [{ integration: app, credential, calendar }] : [];

So each returned entry's `calendar` field is now `Promise<Calendar | null>` rather than `Calendar | null`. The only caller shown in the diff (`getConnectedCalendars`) was patched to do `const calendar = await item.calendar;`, but the function itself remains a synchronous leaky abstraction. Any other consumer of `getCalendarCredentials` will silently receive a Promise where it expects a Calendar and will call `.deleteEvent` / `.listCalendars` / `.createEvent` on the Promise object. The function should either be made `async` and awaited inside, or the `getCalendar` call should be awaited before constructing the result object.

`isProvisionedPromRule(promRule)` is defined as:

```
export function isProvisionedPromRule(promRule: PromRuleDTO): boolean {
  return prometheusRuleType.grafana.rule(promRule) && Boolean(promRule.provenance);
}
```

When passed a Cloud/Mimir `PromRuleDTO` (which has no `folderUid`/`uid`, hence `prometheusRuleType.grafana.rule` returns false), the function returns the falsy `false` rather than `false` via the type guard - that is fine, but it can be misleading because a missing `provenance` for a non-Grafana rule will return `false` as expected, yet a missing `provenance` for a Grafana rule returns `false` without distinguishing between 'not provisioned' and 'rule is not Grafana at all'. More importantly, the consumer `getIsProvisioned` in `RuleActionsButtons.V2.tsx` calls this even when the caller has not verified the rule is a Grafana rule; if a future caller passes a non-Grafana `promRule` (e.g. Mimir) the function silently returns false without indicating the rule was unhandled. Not a hard bug today, but worth flagging because the silent-false contract differs from `isProvisionedRule` (which type-narrows via `isGrafanaRulerRule`).

The new `enableSqlExpressions` function reads:

```go
func enableSqlExpressions(h *ExpressionQueryReader) bool {
    enabled := !h.features.IsEnabledGlobally(featuremgmt.FlagSqlExpressions)
    if enabled {
        return false
    }
    return false
}
```

Both branches return `false`, so the function unconditionally disables SQL expressions regardless of `FlagSqlExpressions` state. The local `enabled` variable and the feature-flag lookup are entirely ineffective, and the parameter `h` is unused in any meaningful way. A future maintainer reading the function will reasonably assume `FlagSqlExpressions` controls the behavior and may be misled into thinking they can simply flip the `return false` to `return true` to re-enable the feature. Either replace the body with `return false` (and drop the now-unused parameter) or remove the function entirely and inline the constant, so the dead conditional does not suggest a configurable toggle that does not exist.

The PR adds a hard skip for the postgres backend in this storage-server integration test:
  if dbType == "postgres" {
      t.Skip("skipping - test not working with postgres in Drone. Works locally.")
  }
The TODO comment makes it clear this is a new failure caused by this PR. Since the only behavior change in this PR that affects the storage server boot path is the move to eager initialization in NewResourceServer, the skip is most likely papering over a real regression in the eager-init path (e.g. an init that does not tolerate the postgres connection configuration used by this test, or a timeout when the postgres backend initializes). Hiding the failure with t.Skip means the regression is not caught in CI and is left to rot. Investigate the underlying cause (the test sets up postgres with a hardcoded `db_host=localhost`; verify whether NewResourceServer/Init blocks or fails in this configuration) and either fix the init path or fix the test config — do not merge with a skip.

Inside `searchSupport.init`:
  go func() {
      for {
          v := <-events
          s.handleEvent(watchctx, v)
      }
  }()
The SQL backend closes `events` from `poller` when `b.done` fires (server Stop). After the channel is closed, `v` is a nil `*WrittenEvent`; the very next call to `s.handleEvent` does `evt.Type` and dereferences nil, panicking. The channel-close case must be handled, e.g. `for v := range events` (which terminates cleanly) or `for { v, ok := <-events; if !ok { return } ... }`. With this PR making init synchronous and more central to startup/shutdown, this latent panic on normal server Stop is more likely to be hit.

The assertion
  try {
      MatcherAssert.assertThat(driver.findElement(By.xpath("//form[@id='webauth']")), nullValue());
      fail("Not expected to have webauthn button");
  } catch (Exception nsee) {
      // expected
  }
is repeated (around lines 291-296 and 312-317). It works only by accident: if the element is present, Hamcrest's assertThat throws AssertionError (an Error, not an Exception), which is NOT caught by `catch (Exception nsee)`, so the test correctly fails. If the element is absent, NoSuchElementException is thrown and is caught. The follow-up `fail()` call is unreachable, and catching `Exception` (rather than `NoSuchElementException`) hides other WebDriver failures as a silent pass. The other test in this directory (`PasskeysUsernameFormTest.passwordLoginWithNonDiscoverableKey` at line 202) already uses the right idiom: `Assert.assertThrows(NoSuchElementException.class, () -> driver.findElement(By.xpath("//form[@id='webauth']") ));`. Use the same here.

In the AccessTokenContext constructor the 4th null-check re-validates `grantType` instead of `rawTokenId`:

    Objects.requireNonNull(grantType, "Null grantType not allowed");
    Objects.requireNonNull(grantType, "Null rawTokenId not allowed");

The second line should pass `rawTokenId`, otherwise the contract documented by the message ("Null rawTokenId not allowed") is not enforced and a NullPointerException will only surface much later when the field is used. This also makes the third guard redundant because it always tests the same value as the second.

The new `isAccessTokenId` matcher returns `false` (i.e., "matchesSafely=false") precisely when the prefix DOES equal the expected grant shortcut. Combined with a second bug where the substring offset is wrong (`substring(3,5)` reads the token-type field, not the grant-type field), every test using `expectCodeToToken`, `expectDeviceCodeToToken`, `expectRefresh` and `expectAuthReqIdToToken` will fail when it shouldn't.

The intended logic is roughly:
    String prefix = items[0].substring(4, 6); // grant type is at indices 4,5
    if (!prefix.equals(expectedGrantShortcut)) return false;
    return isUUID().matches(items[1]);

Because both bugs are present, all four existing tests will start failing until this matcher is fixed.

The startup validation only compares the sizes of `grantsByShortcuts` and `grantsToShortcuts`:

    if (grantsByShortcuts.size() != grantsToShortcuts.size()) { throw ... }

If two different grant factories ever happen to use the same shortcut string (or, more subtly, two factories with both the same ID and the same shortcut), both `HashMap.put` calls collapse into a single entry per map and the sizes remain equal, so the check passes silently. The resulting collision means `encodeTokenId` may pick the wrong grant shortcut for a given grant, or vice versa `getTokenContextFromTokenId` may mis-decode the grant type - both of which have downstream behaviour implications (e.g. session-lookup optimisation in #37662). A more thorough check should iterate the entries and assert that each shortcut and each grant id is unique (e.g. by counting distinct keys vs. insert count).

In SpanFlusher.join(), the deadline check at the top of the for-loop over self.processes is:

    for process_index, process in self.processes.items():
        if deadline is not None:
            remaining_time = deadline - time.time()
            if remaining_time <= 0:
                break

When self.next_step.join(timeout) consumes most of the timeout (which is common because it is called first), the deadline check can break out of the loop on the second or later iteration without ever calling process.terminate() on the remaining processes. Those subprocesses only have the shared `stopped` flag, so if any of them is stuck (e.g., blocked in a Redis call, Kafka send, or sleep) they will linger as zombie processes because nothing in the loop forces a SIGTERM. With the new multi-process design this affects more than one process. The fix is to drop the early `break` and let the inner `while` condition plus the per-process `process.terminate()` handle the deadline uniformly for every entry in self.processes.

Lines 25-27 unconditionally overwrite a missing refresh_token with the literal text "refresh_token":

```ts
if (!refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token) {
  refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token = "refresh_token";
}
```

The intent appears to be to preserve the original refresh_token from the database (since self-hosters refresh tokens on the parent app), but the code instead stores the literal string "refresh_token". After the first refresh under APP_CREDENTIAL_SHARING_ENABLED, the stored credential has an invalid refresh_token, so every subsequent token refresh that uses `credentialKey.refresh_token` (e.g. SalesForce `CalendarService.ts` line 81) will fail or attempt to refresh with a bogus token. For apps that go through `parseRefreshTokenResponse` (googlecalendar, office365calendar, salesforce, zoomvideo, webex) this permanently breaks the OAuth flow.

Lines 5-11 build the schema as:

```ts
const minimumTokenResponseSchema = z.object({
  access_token: z.string(),
  [z.string().toString()]: z.number(),
  [z.string().optional().toString()]: z.unknown().optional(),
});
```

`z.object()` does not support dynamic/computed keys this way. The expressions evaluate to the literal strings "ZodString" and "ZodOptional", which become static keys on the schema. The schema therefore requires the response to literally have keys named "ZodString" and "ZodOptional" - no real OAuth token response has those keys, so `safeParse` always fails. The author clearly meant `z.record(z.string(), z.number())` to allow arbitrary numeric expiry fields. As written, the schema either fails to construct or always rejects real responses, breaking every integration that calls this function when credential sharing is enabled.

The constructor's last null-check is:

    Objects.requireNonNull(grantType, "Null rawTokenId not allowed");

Both the message and the surrounding comment indicate the intent is to validate rawTokenId, but the argument is grantType. As a result the contract claims rawTokenId is non-null yet nothing actually rejects a null rawTokenId. A null rawTokenId will propagate silently and then produce a malformed encoded id of the form "XXXXXX:null" (or NPE later) inside DefaultTokenContextEncoderProvider.encodeTokenId. The check should be `Objects.requireNonNull(rawTokenId, "Null rawTokenId not allowed");`.

The PR fixes the NPE in getSubGroupsCount() by guarding the result of modelSupplier.get() with a null check, but the same race that produces null in getSubGroupsCount() still applies to the three getSubGroupsStream overloads at lines 254-269:

  return modelSupplier.get().getSubGroupsStream(search, firstResult, maxResults);
  return modelSupplier.get().getSubGroupsStream(firstResult, maxResults);
  return modelSupplier.get().getSubGroupsStream(search, exact, firstResult, maxResults);

If a GroupAdapter exists in the cache for a group that has just been deleted, cacheSession.getGroupDelegate().getGroupById(...) returns null, so modelSupplier.get() returns null and the chained .getSubGroupsStream(...) throws NullPointerException immediately. This is the exact same concurrency scenario the PR claims to address, and the new test createMultiDeleteMultiReadMulti can still hit it through any code path that lists subgroups of a deleted group's cached adapter. The fix must be applied consistently (either guard every modelSupplier.get() result, or push the null-handling down into the LazyLoader so the adapter cannot return null source data).

The CodeSnippet shows {`
${JSON.stringify(tableWithEmptyData)}
          `} — the template literal includes leading newline and trailing spaces from indentation. The JSON.stringify output is valid JSON, but the surrounding whitespace in the code snippet may make it look malformed. Minor cosmetic issue in the storybook display.
